# 64-atom diamond-lattice LJ system constrained to P-1 (inversion through
# Cartesian (a/8, a/8, a/8)). Unlike the simple-cubic FCC example, the
# inversion center in diamond sits between atoms rather than on any atom,
# so every orbit is a clean pair (32 pairs, no Wyckoff). The op's t-vector
# (1/(4N), 1/(4N), 1/(4N)) in fractional coordinates is sized for an NxNxN
# cell of conventional diamond cells.
#
# Companion files data.diamond_pinv and diamond_pinv.json are produced by
# generate_diamond_pinv.py.

units           lj
atom_style      atomic
atom_modify     map array

read_data       data.diamond_pinv
Reading data file ...
  orthogonal box = (0 0 0) to (5.2 5.2 5.2)
  1 by 1 by 1 MPI processor grid
  reading atoms ...
  64 atoms
  read_data CPU = 0.000 seconds

pair_style      lj/cut 2.5
pair_coeff      1 1 1.0 1.0 2.5

neighbor        0.3 bin
neigh_modify    every 20 delay 0 check no

velocity        all create 1.2 87287 loop geom

fix             1 all nve
fix             sym all symmetry diamond_pinv.json

thermo          50
thermo_style    custom step temp pe etotal press

# Pick a representative pair (tags 1 and 5 per diamond_pinv.json: tag 1 at
# (0,0,0) and tag 5 at (a/4, a/4, a/4), inversion-related through (a/8,
# a/8, a/8)). The constraint  s_a + s_b == t  (mod 1) in fractional coords
# becomes  x_a + x_b == L/8  (mod L) in Cartesian. Print the residual after
# rounding out any periodic-image translation; this must stay ~1e-12.
variable        Loff equal lx/8
variable        resx equal (x[1]+x[5]-v_Loff)/lx-round((x[1]+x[5]-v_Loff)/lx)
variable        resy equal (y[1]+y[5]-v_Loff)/ly-round((y[1]+y[5]-v_Loff)/ly)
variable        resz equal (z[1]+z[5]-v_Loff)/lz-round((z[1]+z[5]-v_Loff)/lz)

print           "pre-run  pair residual=(${resx},${resy},${resz})"
pre-run  pair residual=(0,0,0)
run             200

